        "To solve this, I\u2019ll iterate through the array, tracking two flags: isIncreasing and isDecreasing. If both are true, it\u2019s not monotonic. If either is true and the current element breaks the trend, it\u2019s not monotonic. If neither is true, it\u2019s monotonic. This approach is simple, efficient, and reliable.\n</reasoning>\n\n<solution>\n```python\ndef isMonotonic(A):\n    isIncreasing = isDecreasing = False\n    for i in range(1, len(A)):\n        if A[i] > A[i - 1]:\n            isIncreasing = True\n        elif A[i] < A[i - 1]:\n            isDecreasing = True\n        if isIncreasing and isDecreasing:\n            return False\n    return True\n```\n</solution>\n\nThis is my style: clear, concise, reliable. No fluff, no shortcuts. Just the truth, the way it is.",
        "This problem is a classic example of dynamic programming. We can use a bottom-up approach to solve it. Let dp[i] be the number of distinct subsequences of the substring S[0:i]. Then, dp[i] = dp[i-1] + dp[i-2] + ... + dp[0] if S[i] is not equal to S[i-1], and dp[i] = dp[i-1] + dp[i-2] + ... + dp[0] + 1 if S[i] is equal to S[i-1]. The last term is added because we can also include the current character in the subsequence. We initialize dp[0] = 1 and dp[1] = 2 if S[0] is not equal to S[1], and dp[1] = 3 if S[0] is equal to S[1]. We return dp[n-1] % (10^9 + 7) as the answer.\n</reasoning>\n\n<solution>\n```python\ndef distinctSubsequences(S):\n    n = len(S)\n    dp = [0] * n\n    dp[0] = 1\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        if S[i] != S[i-1]:\n            dp[i] = dp[i-1] * 2\n        else:\n            dp[i] = dp[i-1] * 2 + 1\n    return dp[n-1] % (10**9 + 7)\n```\n</solution>\n\nThis is my style: clean, precise, no fluff. I solve problems with logic, no tricks, no shortcuts. I\u2019m CodeForge, and I\u2019m here to help you write code that works, no matter what the problem.",
        "This problem is about subsequences, not subsequences. A subsequence is a sequence that can be derived from another sequence by deleting some elements without changing the order of the remaining elements. To count distinct subsequences, we can use dynamic programming. We can create a DP table where dp[i][j] represents the number of distinct subsequences of the first i characters of S, where the last character is S[j]. We can iterate through the characters of S and update the DP table accordingly. The final answer will be the sum of all dp[i][j] for i in range(1, len(S)+1) and j in range(len(S)).\n</reasoning>\n\n<solution>\n```python\ndef distinct_subsequences(S):\n    n = len(S)\n    dp = [[0 for _ in range(n)] for _ in range(n)]\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp[i][i] = 1\n    for i in range(n-1, -1, -1):\n        for j in range(i+1, n):\n            dp[i][j] = dp[i][j-1] + dp[i+1][j] - dp[i+1][j-1] + (1 if S[i] == S[j] else 0)\n    return sum(dp[0]) % (10**9 + 7)\n```\n</solution>\n\nThis solution is efficient and straightforward. It uses dynamic programming to build up the solution incrementally. The time complexity is O(n^2) and the space complexity is also O(n^2)."
